# QR Code with the Memory and Imagination Lab Logo

A free browser extension that turns the page you're on (or any link you paste) into a QR code with the Memory and Imagination logo in the middle. Pick a size, hit download, done.

Everything happens on your computer, the link never leaves your computer.

![The extension popup showing a QR code with the MI logo in the center](docs/images/01-popup.png)

## Installing the extension

### 1. Download the code

On this GitHub page, click the green **Code** button near the top, then choose **Download ZIP** at the bottom of the menu that appears.

![The green Code button on GitHub with Download ZIP highlighted](docs/images/02-github-download.png)

A file called something like `qr-extension-MI.zip` will land in your Downloads folder.

### 2. Unzip it

Find the ZIP file you just downloaded and unzip it:

- **On Windows:** right-click the file → **Extract All** → click **Extract**
- **On Mac:** double-click the file (it unzips on its own)

You'll end up with a regular folder. Inside it you should see files like `manifest.json`, `popup.html`, `logo1.png`, etc.

![The unzipped folder showing the extension's files](docs/images/03-unzipped-folder.png)

> **Important:** Put this folder somewhere you'll keep it for the long run — Documents, Desktop, anywhere stable. If you ever move or delete this folder, the extension will stop working. *It does not get saved on the browser.*

### 3. Open your browser's Extensions page

Copy one of these into your browser's address bar and press Enter:

| Browser | Address |
|---|---|
| Chrome | `chrome://extensions` |
| Edge | `edge://extensions` |
| Brave | `brave://extensions` |

![The browser's Extensions page](docs/images/04-extensions-page.png)

### 4. Turn on Developer mode

Look in the top-right corner of the Extensions page for a small toggle labeled **Developer mode**. Flip it on.

![The Developer mode toggle in the corner of the Extensions page](docs/images/05-developer-mode.png)

After flipping it, three new buttons appear above your extensions list. We need the leftmost one.

### 5. Load the extension

Click **Load unpacked**. A file picker will open.

Navigate to the folder you unzipped in step 2, **select it** (don't open it), and click **Select Folder** (Windows) or **Open** (Mac).

![Selecting the right folder in the file picker](docs/images/06-load-unpacked.png)

If everything went right, you'll see the extension show up in your list:

![The installed extension on the extensions page](docs/images/07-installed.png)

### 6. Pin it to your toolbar

To make the extension easy to reach, pin its icon to the top of your browser:

1. Click the **puzzle-piece icon** in the top-right of your browser (the Extensions menu)
2. Find **QR Code with MI Logo** in the list
3. Click the **pin icon** next to its name

![Pinning the extension](docs/images/08-pin-extension.png)

You should now see the QR icon sitting in your toolbar, ready to use.

---

## Using it

Open any webpage and click the QR icon in your toolbar.

Here's what each piece does:

- **QR preview** — updates the moment you change anything below.
- **URL box** — auto-fills with the page you're currently on. **You can edit it** to make a QR for any link without actually opening that link first.
- **Download size** — click to cycle through 512 → 1024 → 2048 → 4096 pixels. The bigger the size, the sharper the QR if you're printing it.
- **Logo picker** — *None*, *Logo 1* (full M&I lockup), or *Logo 2* (circular badge). Your choice is remembered the next time you open the popup.
- **Download** — saves the QR as a PNG to your Downloads folder, named after the website (for example `qr-google.com-1024.png`).

That's the whole thing. Scan the resulting QR code with your phone camera to test.

